### What are the key differences?

| Aspect | `collect(toList())` | `toArray(T[]::new)` |
|---|---|---|
| Result type | `List<T>` (mutable ArrayList) | `T[]` (fixed-size array) |
| Generics | Full support | Limited (arrays and generics mix poorly) |
| Modification | Resizable, add/remove | Fixed size, only replace elements |
| Composability | Re-stream, Collections API | Manual iteration |
| Use when | Most application code | API requires array, varargs, primitives |

### When should you use toArray?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class ToArrayVsToList {

    record Employee(String id, String name, BigDecimal salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("SAV2001", "Rajesh",  new BigDecimal("1800000")),
            new Employee("SAV2002", "Priya",   new BigDecimal("1200000")),
            new Employee("SAV2003", "Meera",   new BigDecimal("1500000"))
        );

        // Use case 1: API requires an array
        Employee[] array = staff.stream().toArray(Employee[]::new);
        legacyReport(array);

        // Use case 2: Primitive array without boxing
        int[] salaryInts = staff.stream()
            .mapToInt(e -> e.salary().intValue())
            .toArray();  // int[], no boxing

        // Default: use toList for everything else
        List<Employee> list = staff.stream()
            .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1300000")) > 0)
            .toList();
    }

    static void legacyReport(Employee[] employees) {
        System.out.println("Legacy report: " + employees.length + " employees");
    }
}
```

### What about Java 16's Stream.toList()?

```java
// Collectors.toList(): returns mutable ArrayList
List<String> mutable = staff.stream().map(Employee::name)
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toList());
mutable.add("NewName");  // works

// Stream.toList() (Java 16+): returns unmodifiable list
List<String> unmodifiable = staff.stream().map(Employee::name).toList();
// unmodifiable.add("NewName");  // throws UnsupportedOperationException
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Using toArray when a List would be simpler.

```java
// WRONG: unnecessary array conversion
Employee[] arr = staff.stream().filter(predicate).toArray(Employee[]::new);
for (Employee e : arr) { process(e); }

// CORRECT: just use a List
List<Employee> list = staff.stream().filter(predicate).toList();
list.forEach(this::process);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Default to List for flexibility. Reserve toArray for: (1) primitive performance via primitive-stream toArray, (2) API/varargs interoperability, and (3) fixed-size contracts. Converting a list to an array later is trivial if needed. Know the difference between `Collectors.toList()` (mutable) and `Stream.toList()` (unmodifiable).

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between Collectors.toList() and Stream.toList()?" Mutability: Collectors gives ArrayList, Stream.toList() gives unmodifiable.
- "When does toArray outperform toList?" Rarely: the difference is negligible for reference types. Primitive streams benefit from toArray returning int[]/long[] without boxing.
- "Can you convert a list to an array?" Yes: `list.toArray(Employee[]::new)`.
